  "categoryBlurbs": {
    "Arrays & Strings": "The highest-frequency surface in any interview loop. Master the four pattern families here before anything else.",
    "Hashing, Stacks & Queues": "O(1) lookup and LIFO/FIFO discipline — the structures that turn an O(n^2) scan into O(n).",
    "Linked Lists": "Pointer surgery. Low conceptual depth, high implementation-precision bar.",
    "Trees & Heaps": "Recursive structure plus ordered access. Roughly a quarter of all tree/heap-tagged interview questions come from these five sheets.",
    "Graphs": "Traversal, connectivity and ordering. BFS/DFS are must-know; the weighted shortest-path trio is mostly L5+ territory.",
    "Search & Sort": "Halving a monotonic space, and what sorting buys you. Binary search on the answer is the single most under-practised tier-5 skill.",
    "Dynamic Programming & Recursion": "The hardest tier-5 block. Start from recursion, memoise, then tabulate.",
    "Greedy & Intervals": "Sort-then-scan, and the exchange argument that proves it correct.",
    "Range Queries & Advanced Structures": "Reach for these only when a problem needs updates *and* range queries — otherwise a prefix sum is enough.",
    "Math & Bits": "Small, self-contained topics. Cheap to learn, occasionally decisive.",
    "Design & Simulation": "\"Design a X\" and low-level-design rounds — judged on operation complexity and class modelling, not cleverness.",
    "Language Toolkit": "Language mechanics, not algorithms. Read for speed and to stop losing submissions to avoidable traps.",
    "Interview Meta": "How to triage a question and state its complexity. Read before your first mock."
  },

  "startHere": [
